ecm_tracker: reject a total length below the IP header length

ecm_tracker_ip_check_header_and_read() validates the IPv4 header two
ways - ihl at least 20, and skb->len at least tot_len - and then computes

	remain = ip_hdr->total_length - ip_hdr->ip_header_length;

into a uint16_t. Nothing relates the two operands, so a frame whose
tot_len is smaller than its ihl underflows remain to nearly 64 KB, and
that value is handed to every per-protocol header helper as the amount of
packet left to parse.

Both operands are attacker-chosen and the two existing checks pass
happily: ihl = 15 (60 bytes) with tot_len = 40 satisfies "ihl >= 20" and
"skb->len >= tot_len" on any ordinary frame.

One raw Ethernet frame from any bridged LAN or Wi-Fi client reaches it -
ECM inspects every flow it might accelerate.

Bound the subtraction by its own operands, next to the checks that
already guard it. The IPv6 branch takes remain straight from
payload_len and does not subtract, so it has no sibling defect.

Found by a coverage-guided ASan harness over the tracker entry point.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

--- a/ecm_tracker.c
+++ b/ecm_tracker.c
@@ -465,6 +465,10 @@ version_check_done:
 			DEBUG_WARN("%px: v4 invalid total len: %u skb len: %u\n", skb, ip_hdr->total_length, skb->len);
 			return false;
 		}
+		if (ip_hdr->total_length < ip_hdr->ip_header_length) {
+			DEBUG_WARN("%px: v4 total len %u below ip hdr len %u\n", skb, ip_hdr->total_length, ip_hdr->ip_header_length);
+			return false;
+		}
 		remain = ip_hdr->total_length - ip_hdr->ip_header_length;
 
 		/*
